    {
      "id": "qtm-m5-worked-examples-c3",
      "heading": "Coffee bags: set-up and test statistic",
      "explanation_md": "**Problem:** a machine fills coffee bags that should each weigh **1 kg**. A random sample of **30 bags** has a mean of **0.98 kg**, with a standard deviation of **0.05 kg**. Test at a **5% significance level** whether the average bag weight **differs** from 1 kg.\n\n**Step 1: Hypotheses.**\n\nH0: mean weight = 1 kg\n\nH1: mean weight ≠ 1 kg (**two-tailed test**)\n\n**Step 2: Significance level.** α = 0.05, **split into 0.025 in each tail**.\n\n**Step 3: Test statistic.** The slide uses a **t statistic**:\n\nt = (x̄ − μ0) / (s/√n) = (0.98 − 1) / (0.05/√30) = −0.02/0.00913 ≈ **−2.19**\n\n**Degrees of freedom: df = n − 1 = 29.**",
      "example_ids": [
        "qtm-ex-m5-coffee-bags"
      ],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Degrees of freedom (df)",
          "meaning": "For this t test, df = n − 1 (here 29).",
          "source_ref": "qtm--module-5#30"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "Coffee: H0: mean = 1 kg, H1: mean ≠ 1 kg; t = (0.98 − 1)/(0.05/√30) = −0.02/0.00913 ≈ −2.19; df = n − 1 = 29.",
      "clarification": null,
